        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 马路",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "马路 — mǎlù — \"road; a street for traffic\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我家在马路对面 wǒ jiā zài mǎlù duìmiàn — My home is across the road."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 路 (lù) — thirteen strokes in two halves: on the left the foot piece, 口 over four short strokes; on the right a short fall, a bar that turns and falls to the left, a long stroke falling to the right, and 口 under them. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "马路 (mǎlù) is built from 马 'horse' and 路 (lù) 'road', both inherited from Middle Chinese, maeX and luH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). Why a road for traffic is named after horses is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for asking where things are."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
